{} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} " 8 August 2012"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/861"} "ExCeL, Newham, London"] " (North Arena 2)"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/332736"} "20 from 20 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Single elimination tournament. Competitors losing to the finalists entered repechage tournament for bronze medals."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "There was only one favorite in the women’s middleweight freestyle wrestling event at the 2012 Summer Olympics: " [:a {:href "/athletes/107562"} "Kaori Icho"] " of Japan. Aside from a forfeit at the 2007 Asian Championships, the Japanese wrestler had won her last 149 matches, held the only two Olympic crowns in the event (2004 and 2008), and was a seven-time World Champion, having won the event in every tournament since 2002, with the exception of the oddly-held 2008 edition and 2009, when she had taken a break from wrestling. No other competitor came close to matching her dominance. She breezed through the tournament to meet " [:a {:href "/athletes/122818"} "Jing Ruixue"] " of China in the final, who had been World Champion in the non-Olympic 67 kg division in 2006 and 2007, runner-up in the middleweight in 2005, and a bronze medalist at the most recent edition. The battle for the crown was almost no contest: Icho had the first round won by the first thirty seconds, having already scored three points on Jing. In the second period, Icho reversed a near-takedown by the leg to score one point and scored another in the final twenty seconds, giving Japan both wrestling medals of the day, following " [:a {:href "/athletes/120610"} "Hitomi Obara"] "’s earlier victory in the flyweight division."] "\n\n" [:p {} "The bronze medal matches were somewhat less one-sided. Canadian " [:a {:href "/athletes/117011"} "Martine Dugrenier"] ", thrice World Champion (2008, 2009, and 2010) and thrice runner-up (2005, 2006, 2007) in the 67 kg category, fought in her second Olympics as a middleweight competitor, but had the misfortune of drawing against Icho in the first round. In the battle for the bronze medal she put up a good fight against " [:a {:href "/athletes/123840"} "Soronzonboldyn Battsetseg"] " of Mongolia, the 2010 World Champion in the 59 kg class (also non-Olympic), but was unable to match her opponent’s skill or score any points, losing 0-2 and 0-1 and giving Mongolia its first Olympic medal in wrestling since 1980. The battle for the final podium spot was perhaps the most hard fought, pitting " [:a {:href "/athletes/117013"} "Monika Michalik"] " of Poland, twice bronze-medalist at the World Championships (2006 and 2007) and thrice gold medalist at the European Championships (2003 in the 59 kg division, 2005, 2009), against " [:a {:href "/athletes/120350"} "Lyubov Volosova"] " of Russia, three-time World Championship runner-up (2001 in the 56 kg class, 2008, 2009) and once bronze medalist (2010) and twice European Champion (2006 at 56 kg, 2010). The first round went to " [:em {} "par terre"] ", where Michalik drew the advantaged position and scored on her opponent. Volosova had her revenge, however, outscoring the Polish wrestler in the second period 3-1 and converting the advantage in a third-round " [:em {} "par terre"] " to win the bronze."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/107562"} "Kaori Icho"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/JPN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/JPN.png"}] "JPN"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/122818"} "Jing Ruixue"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/C